---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE_NAME, SUBJECTS, syllabusPhrase } from '../config';

/** ", " between subjects and " and " before the last. */
const joiner = (index: number) => (index === 0 ? '' : index === SUBJECTS.length - 1 ? ' and ' : ', ');
const subjectList = SUBJECTS.map((subject, index) => joiner(index) + subject.fullName).join('');
---
<BaseLayout
  title={`About ${SITE_NAME}`}
  description={`What ${SITE_NAME} is: free, original exam-style topical questions for ${subjectList}, with mark schemes and worked solutions, how they were made, and how to report an error.`}
  canonicalPath="/about/"
>
  <div class="shell">
    <article class="prose">
      <h1>About {SITE_NAME}</h1>
      <p>
        {SITE_NAME} is a free collection of exam-style practice questions for Cambridge IGCSE and Pearson
        Edexcel International GCSE subjects, sorted by topic:
      </p>
      <ul>
        {SUBJECTS.map((subject) => <li><a href={`/${subject.path}/`}>{subject.fullName}</a>, {syllabusPhrase(subject)}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p>
        Every question comes with a mark scheme and a worked solution, hidden until you choose to look, so
        you can attempt the question first and then mark your own answer.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the questions come from</h2>
      <p>
        The questions are original. They are written in the style of the exam so that practising them
        feels like the real thing, but they are not past papers, and nothing is copied from Cambridge or
        Pearson Edexcel question papers or mark schemes.
      </p>
      <p>
        The questions, mark schemes and worked solutions were written with AI tools and approved by the site owner. Approval does not claim independent science verification. No teacher
        or examiner has reviewed them. Mistakes are possible: if anything looks wrong, use the Report an
        error link under that question.
      </p>

      <h2>Tiers and papers</h2>
      <p>
        Every Cambridge IGCSE question is tagged Core or Extended, as in the Cambridge tiers. Core questions are for everyone.
        Extended questions use Supplement content and are only for students taking the Extended papers
        (Papers 2 and 4). On a topic page, Extended shows every question, Core shows the Core questions only,
        and Extended content only shows just the harder Supplement material.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pearson Edexcel International GCSE has no tiers, so its questions are labelled Paper 1 &amp; 2 or
        Paper 2 only instead. Paper 2 only questions use the bold content in the specification, which is
        examined only on Paper 2.
      </p>

      <h2>Reporting an error</h2>
      <p>
        Errors are reported through the Report an error form under each question. It opens with the
        question's id and web address already filled in, so you only need to say what looks wrong. Nothing
        on the form identifies you unless you choose to add your details.
      </p>
      <p>Reports are read, and corrections are published to the site.</p>

      <h2>Independence</h2>
      <p>
        {SITE_NAME} is independent and is not affiliated with or endorsed by Cambridge University Press
        &amp; Assessment or Pearson Education. The qualifications are named only to say what the questions
        are for.
      </p>

      <h2>Free, with no accounts</h2>
      <p>
        Everything on the site is free to use. There is nothing to sign up for and nothing to download.
        The site keeps no record of what you answer; your score on a page lasts only as long as the page is
        open. See the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> for the full list, which is short.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
